      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "8aeae034-863f-54e3-9502-21836c0aea43",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: New York, Lake Champlain, Hudson River, Warren County, Lebanon, Middletown, southwestern Ohio, Glens Falls\n- Systems: Champlain Canal, Warren County Canal, Miami and Erie Canal, Glens Falls Feeder Canal\n\nFacts:\n- The Warren County Canal was a mid-19th-century branch of the Miami and Erie Canal in southwestern Ohio.\n- The Warren County Canal was about 20 miles long and connected Lebanon, the seat of Warren County, to the main canal at Middletown.\n- The Champlain Canal is a 60-mile canal in New York.\n- The Champlain Canal connects the south end of Lake Champlain to the Hudson River.\n- The Glens Falls Feeder Canal is 7 miles long.\n- The Glens Falls Feeder Canal delivers water from above Glens Falls on the Hudson River to the highest point of the Champlain Canal.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
